### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient M.R.
* Age: 64 years
* Gender: Female
* Weight: 68 kg
* Medical History: Dyslipidemia (5 years), Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (8 years), systemic fungal infection (newly diagnosed).

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1: Patient stabilized on Rosuvastatin 20 mg once daily for dyslipidemia.
* Month 6 (Day 1): Diagnosed with systemic candidiasis; initiated on Fluconazole 200 mg once daily.
* Month 6 (Day 6): Patient developed bilateral severe upper arm and thigh muscle weakness, diffuse myalgia, and generalized fatigue.
* Month 6 (Day 9): Patient observed tea-colored (dark reddish-brown) urine and presented to the emergency department.
* Laboratory Findings at Admission:
  * Serum Creatine Kinase (CK): 14,500 U/L (Normal reference range: 30 to 135 U/L)
  * Serum Creatinine: 2.8 mg/dL (Baseline: 0.9 mg/dL)
  * Urinalysis: Positive for myoglobin, negative for RBCs.
* Intervention: Immediate permanent cessation of Rosuvastatin and Fluconazole; aggressive IV fluid resuscitation (0.9 percent Normal Saline) and urinary alkalinization initiated.
* Follow-up (Day 18 post-discontinuation): Complete resolution of myalgia, normalization of urine color, serum CK reduced to 180 U/L, and serum creatinine restored to baseline (1.0 mg/dL).

